      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given $f(x)=3x^{3}-6x$ and $x\\ne 0$, calculate the integral.\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{f(x)}{x^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{x^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int \\frac{3x^{3}-6x}{x^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(3 - \\frac{6}{x^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=3\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x-6\\int x^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=3\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)-6\\left(\\frac{x^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C \\\\ &=3x+\\frac{6}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe blank excludes the constant $C$, which is already supplied.",
